{"name":"Spray","hex":"#7ecddd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spray","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine, gentle, dispersed mist of liquid.","Something resembling a spray of liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To project a liquid in a dispersive manner toward something.","To project in a dispersive manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spray"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spray (liquid drop)","blocks":[{"text":"A spray is a dynamic collection of drops dispersed in a gas. The process of forming a spray is known as atomization. A spray nozzle is the device used to generate a spray. The two main uses of sprays are to distribute material over a cross-section and to generate liquid surface area. There are thousands of applications in which sprays allow material to be used most efficiently. The spray characteristics required must be understood in order to select the most appropriate technology, optimal device and size."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Aerosol.png/330px-Aerosol.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spray_(liquid_drop)","wikidata":"Q1424833"},{"title":"Aerosol spray dispenser","blocks":[{"text":"Aerosol spray is a type of dispensing system which creates an aerosol mist of liquid particles. It comprises a can or bottle that contains a payload, and a propellant under pressure. When the container's valve is opened, the payload is forced out of a small opening and emerges as an aerosol or mist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Aerosol.png/330px-Aerosol.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerosol_spray_dispenser","wikidata":"Q659495"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Spray_patterns.png/500px-Spray_patterns.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Spray_patterns.png/500px-Spray_patterns.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flat fan, solid cone and hollow cone spray patterns","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpray_patterns.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/MultiplePlumes.png/250px-MultiplePlumes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/MultiplePlumes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/MultiplePlumes.png/250px-MultiplePlumes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Multiple plume spray","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMultiplePlumes.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Spray_coverage.png/500px-Spray_coverage.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Spray_coverage.png/500px-Spray_coverage.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spray coverage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpray_coverage.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Size_distribution_wiki.png/330px-Size_distribution_wiki.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Size_distribution_wiki.png/330px-Size_distribution_wiki.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cumulative drop size distribution graph","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASize_distribution_wiki.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/